{"content":{"result":[{"rank_order":1,"title":"Resiliency assessments","context":"# Resiliency assessments\n\nA resiliency policy defines the resiliency in terms of recovery point objective (RPO)\nand\nrecovery time objective (RTO) metrics to target for your application and the AWS Cloud\ninfrastructure that supports it.\n\nAfter running a resiliency assessment, you can review the report that shows the gap,\nif\nany, between the estimated and target recovery times. You can identify the application\nusing the\nfollowing:\n\n* **Name** – Name of the application that you had provided while\n defining it in AWS Resilience Hub.\n* **Status** – Indicates the assessment status of the\n application.\n* **Compliance status** – AWS Resilience Hub checks if your application is\n compliant with the policies attached to the application. That is, it checks if the\n application\n and its Application Components (AppComponents) have been estimated to meet recovery\n (RTO and\n RPO) targets defined in the resiliency policy. Additionally, it also checks for application's\n readiness towards adhering to the relevant set of controls that are defined within\n compliance\n readiness policy. You can identify the same using the following:\n\n + **Policy met** – Indicates that your application\n and its AppComponents comply with either one or both the attached policies.\n + **Policy breached** – Indicates that your\n application does not comply with either one or both the attached policies.\n + **Not assessed** – Indicates that your application\n is not assessed against either one or both the attached policies.\n + **Changes detected** – AWS Resilience Hub has detected a newer published\n version of the application. Run a new assessment to determine if your application\n either\n meets your resiliency policy's objectives or adheres with the compliance readiness\n policy,\n or both.\n* **Resiliency** – AWS Resilience Hub checks if your application has been\n assessed against the recovery targets (RTO and RPO) defined in your resiliency policy.\n You can\n identify the same using the following:\n\n + **Policy met** – Indicates that the application and\n its AppComponents have been estimated to meet recovery (RTO and RPO) targets defined\n in the\n resiliency policy.\n + **Policy breached** – Indicates that the\n application could not be estimated to\n\n*[truncated — additional content omitted]*","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/help-panel/resilience-hub/latest/console/resiliency-assessment-compliance-gated.html"},{"rank_order":2,"title":"Enhance Amazon EKS Containerized Application Resilience with AWS Resilience Hub | AWS Cloud Operations Blog","context":"### **Reviewing your first Resiliency assessment and recommendations**\n\nThe Resiliency assessment provides an overview of the assessment report. AWS Resilience Hub lists each disruption type and the associated application component. It also lists your actual RTO and RPO policies and determines whether the application component can achieve the policy goals.\n\nTo review your assessment, follow the below steps\n\n* After the assessment status changes to “Success”. Click on report sock-shop-res-assess\n* Next to the assessment name, you will see either the “Policy met” or “Policy breached”. If you followed the above blog instructions it will be “Policy breached”. Click on the report sock-shop-res-assess assessment.\n\nFigure 7: AWS Resilience Hub assessment\n\n* The report is broken primarily in 3 sections/tabs. The Results, Resiliency recommendations and Operational recommendations. The Results tab lists the summary of the RTO and RPO, Estimated against the Targeted. The results also provides detailed descriptions of each disruption type (application, infrastructure, Availability Zone, and Region).\n\nFigure 8: AWS Resilience Hub Recommendations\n\nAs you see above, AWS Resilience Hub has identified 14 breaches each across Infrastructure, Availability Zone and Region.\n\n* Lets expand into Infrastructure breaches.. Toggle the Infrastructure tab\n* Click on the Estimated RTO for the top AppComponent. A pop up text explains in detail the reason for the AppComponent breach. Feel free to explore the other AppComponent’s in this list\n\nFigure 9: AWS Resilience Hub AppComponent Recommendations\n\n* Now that we have looked at the breaches, lets look at the Resiliency recommendations to fix the policy breaches. Resiliency recommendations evaluate application components and recommend optimization changes by RTO and RPO, costs, and minimal changes.\n* Click on Resiliency recommendations tab\n\nFigure 10: AWS Resilience Hub Resiliency Recommendations\n\n* Under AppComponents, select the top component. You will see the benefits for fixing the AppComponent. For this selection you will see “Optimize for Cost, minimal changes and Best Region RTO/RPO” as the benefits. The Recommendation also suggests Changes to fix the policy compliance. In this example, there are 8 changes recommended to address the application readiness.\n\nFigure 11: AWS Resilience Hub Resiliency Recommendations for EKS resources","url":"https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/mt/enhance-amazon-eks-containerized-application-resilience-with-aws-resilience-hub/"},{"rank_order":3,"title":"AWS Resilience Hub – Resilience management","context":"# AWS Resilience Hub – Resilience management\n\nAWS Resilience Hub gives you a central place to define, validate, and track the resiliency of your\nAWS application. AWS Resilience Hub helps you to protect your applications from disruptions, and reduce\nrecovery costs to optimize business continuity to help meet compliance and regulatory\nrequirements. You can use AWS Resilience Hub to do the following:\n\n* Analyze your infrastructure and get recommendations to improve the resiliency of your\n applications. In addition to architectural guidance for improving your application resiliency,\n the recommendations provide code for meeting your resiliency policy, implementing tests,\n alarms, and standard operating procedures (SOPs) that you can deploy and run with your\n application in your integration and delivery (CI/CD) pipeline.\n* Evaluate recovery time objective (RTO) and recovery point objective (RPO) targets under\n different conditions.\n* Optimize business continuity while reducing recovery costs.\n* Identify and resolve issues before they occur in production.\n\nAfter you deploy an application into production, you can add AWS Resilience Hub to your CI/CD\npipeline to validate every build before it is released into production","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/resilience-hub/latest/userguide/arh-mgmt.html"}]}}

